Session Report: SystemVerilog SNN Tile Testbench Development
1. Task or Question Worked On
[My decision / project work]
I worked on creating and refining a self-checking SystemVerilog testbench for the Team 2 digital SNN tile, block2_digital_snn. The goal was to verify the functionality of the block before the final neuron-selection scheme and final threshold/reset/weight values are fixed.
The main behaviors I wanted to test were:
- whether all 32 neurons can be updated,
- whether positive and negative weights are handled correctly,
- whether threshold crossings generate spikes,
- whether post-spike reset behavior works,
- whether the design blocks new input while busy,
- whether outgoing AER backpressure is handled correctly,
- whether minimum and maximum signed weight cases behave correctly.
2. Main Steps in My Reasoning
[My reasoning / decisions]
I decided that the testbench should focus on functional verification rather than final parameter tuning. Because neuron selection is still unresolved, I treated the current neuron-index mapping only as a temporary mechanism to prove that each of the 32 neuron-state entries is accessible.
I also decided that a single DUT instance was not enough because weight is a module parameter. To test positive and negative behavior in the same simulation, I needed multiple DUT instances with different parameter values.
I identified four useful test configurations:
- a normal positive-weight DUT,
- a normal negative-weight DUT,
- a maximum signed positive-weight DUT,
- a minimum signed negative-weight DUT.
I also wanted the testbench to be self-checking, so the testbench compares expected and actual behavior instead of relying only on manual waveform inspection.
[AI-generated assistance]
The AI helped translate these goals into directed test cases and suggested using multiple parameterized DUT instances so that positive, negative, minimum, and maximum weight cases could all be tested in one simulation.
