[AI-generated assistance]
The AI produced a self-checking testbench structure and later rewrote it to use multiple DUT instances with different parameter values.
4. Important AI Responses or Suggestions
[AI-generated]
The AI suggested the following four DUT configurations:
- dut_pos with W = +1,
- dut_neg with W = -1,
- dut_max with W = +127,
- dut_min with W = -128.
The AI suggested using arbitrary but convenient threshold/reset values strictly for verification so that the block could be driven through both non-spiking and spiking cases.
The AI also suggested using hierarchical checks such as dut.V_M[n] because the current block does not expose neuron membrane voltage through a functional output port. This allows the testbench to directly verify that each neuron state was actually updated.
The AI also identified an RTL-specific limitation in the maximum-positive test: because threshold detection occurs before the positive saturation branch, the +127 case is best interpreted as a maximum signed-weight threshold/spike/reset test rather than a direct positive-saturation test.
5. What I Accepted, Changed, or Rejected
[My decisions]
I accepted the multi-DUT architecture because it allows all required weight-sign and boundary cases to be exercised in one testbench.
I accepted the use of internal neuron-state signals for verification because otherwise it would be difficult to prove that all 32 neurons are being updated correctly.
I accepted arbitrary verification-only parameter values because the current goal is to validate functionality rather than finalize the biological model.
I kept the four-DUT setup and did not add additional DUT instances because the positive, negative, maximum, and minimum cases already cover the required functional categories.
I also kept the testbench self-checking so that it can automatically report whether each directed test passes or fails.
6. Remaining Questions or Limitations
[My assessment / unresolved items]
The final neuron-selection and AER address mapping is still unresolved. The current testbench only proves that all 32 neuron-state entries are accessible under the temporary mapping.
The threshold, reset voltage, beta value, and weight values used by the testbench are verification values and are not intended to define the final design.
The testbench uses white-box checks on internal signals such as V_M, so it is not purely interface-based verification.
The maximum positive-weight test verifies correct threshold crossing, spike generation, and reset behavior, but it does not independently exercise positive saturation because of the current ordering of the RTL logic.
